    "result_text": "I implemented `search_remote` in `kb/remote.py`. It connects to the server at `url` over Streamable HTTP and sends `Authorization: Bearer <token>` on every request. It calls the `search` tool with `{\"query\": query}` and returns titles using the same `split_titles` function as `search_local`.\n\n```python\nasync def search_remote(url: str, token: str, query: str) -> list[str]:\n    headers = {\"Authorization\": f\"Bearer {token}\"}\n    async with httpx2.AsyncClient(headers=headers, timeout=TIMEOUT) as http_client:\n        async with streamable_http_client(url, http_client=http_client) as (read, write):\n            async with ClientSession(read, write) as session:\n                await session.initialize()\n                result = await session.call_tool(\"search\", {\"query\": query})\n    return split_titles(result)\n```\n\n**Verification:** `pytest -q` passes (2 tests), but those only cover `split_titles`. So I also ran a one-off check against a local server that recorded the headers of every HTTP request. It returned `['Title A for vpn', 'Title B']`. All six requests carried `Bearer s3cret`: four POSTs, the GET and the DELETE that closes the session.\n\n**Choices:**\n- In the installed mcp 2.2.0, `streamable_http_client` no longer takes a `headers=` argument. Instead you give it your own `httpx2.AsyncClient`, and I set the header on that client.\n- `httpx2` is already a dependency of mcp 2.2.0, so I added nothing to `pyproject.toml`.\n- mcp's own helper for building this client is in a private module (`mcp.shared._httpx_utils`), so I built the client directly. I set the same timeouts the SDK uses by default: 30s, and 300s for reads, because the server can keep a response stream open.\n\n**Cleanup needed:** the check script `_e2e_kb.py` is still in the project root. Deleting it needed your approval and didn't go through, so please delete it yourself."
